-## The step file
+## Step files
 
-One check per file, one runner call, tiny output. Lines are trimmed; blank lines and `#` comments are
-ignored.
+Blank lines and `#` comments are ignored. `cmd:` runs one command. `expect:` checks the previous output. `text:` retries non-interactive snapshots for literal text. `ref name = literal` finds a matching snapshot line and captures its `[ref=eN]`; later commands can use `@name`. `batch:` through `end` runs a recursively screened `batch --bail`. Do not add inline comments to command lines.
 
 ```
-# comment
-batch:                              # the lines up to `end` become one `batch --bail` call
-  open http://127.0.0.1:3111/pair/abc123
-  set viewport 390 844
-end
-ref bot = button "Developer"        # re-snapshot until a line contains this text and has [ref=eN]
-cmd: click @bot                     # @bot is replaced with the captured ref
+cmd: open http://127.0.0.1:3111/
+cmd: set viewport 390 844
 ref start = button "Start chat"
 cmd: click @start
-ref box = textbox "Message Developer"
-cmd: fill @box "hello runner"
-ref send = button "Send"
-cmd: click @send
-text: hello runner                  # re-snapshot (non-interactive) until this text is on the page
-expect: hello runner                # assert against the previous command's output, no new call
+text: Start a conversation
 cmd: screenshot /tmp/evidence/phone.png
 cmd: console
 cmd: errors
 cmd: close
 ```
 
-Rules that keep it honest:
-
-- `ref <name> = <literal text>` matches a line that *contains* that text and captures its `[ref=eN]`.
-  It retries `--tries` times with `--wait-ms` between attempts, then fails the step file with exit 3.
-  Never guess a ref across a navigation; capture it again.
-- `text: <literal>` uses a non-interactive snapshot, because interactive snapshots omit ordinary
-  message text. Use it to prove a message or reply is really on the page.
-- `expect: <literal>` asserts against the previous command's own output and costs no extra call.
-- A `batch:` block is for steps you already know in sequence. `--bail` stops it at the first error.
-- The last step should be `cmd: close`. The runner also closes in its `finally` block either way.
-
-## Troubleshooting
-
-| Symptom | Cause and fix |
-|---|---|
-| The call seems to hang and never returns | You wrapped it in a shell pipeline. The daemon inherits the pipe handle. Call the runner without `\| tee`, `\| cat` or `2>&1 \| …`; redirect to a file or use `--out <log>`. |
-| `REFUSED - flag '…' is banned` | Use the runner's own modes; those flags attach to a real browser or a cloud account. |
-| `REFUSED - a project config exists here` | You are in a folder with `agent-browser.json`. Run from your evidence folder. |
-| `no element matching '…' after N bounded snapshots` | The element is deeper than `--depth`, still hydrating, or genuinely absent. Try `--depth 10`, then a scoped `snapshot -s <selector>`, before reporting a bug. |
-| A run was killed or timed out | Close your session (`--close`), then remove its files (`--sweep --session <name>`). Never sweep other sessions. |
-| `close` left `<session>.config` / `<session>.target` | They are metadata, not a live browser. `--close` removes your own; `--sweep --session <name>` clears a dead one. |
-| The pin check refuses | The exe or the clean config changed. Stop and tell the CTO; do not edit either to make it pass. |
+A step file always closes in `finally`, including a failed assertion. Never guess refs across navigation. Text/ref retries are bounded. Exit codes: 0 success; 2 policy refusal; 3 command/assertion failure; 4 timeout (captured CLI PID killed); 5 internal/pin error. After an interrupted single command use `--close`, then `--sweep` for dead metadata. The idle timeout is a backstop, not permission to leave a session running.
